WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.159
Pete: So tell me about the next layer of your
architecture.

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:08.780
You're doing some interesting things on the
data side using NoSQL technologies and other

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.780
things.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:10.780
Talk to me a little bit about that.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.620
Dag: For every single bid request we get in
we actually have to do some sort of storage

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.710
lookup.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:24.640
And the way that is done, actually we have
several, usually two look-ups per request.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:33.000
So, if we're doing 25,000 requests per second,
then we have to do about 50,000 look-ups.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:40.160
And also, we do store information about them,
so we have also fairly substantial write throughput.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:47.820
And this is like one of those obvious situations
where traditional, very general, database

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.690
technologies fall through.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:54.170
We could probably scale the reads pretty well
up to that amount.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:56.320
But the writes would be very, very hard.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:04.260
And also, because we're so latency constrained,
having a general database engine to add 2-3

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.530
extra milliseconds is just a waste of time.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:11.280
Pete: So because of this, how many different
types of data stores do you have?

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.780
Dag: So, we have three types basically.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:21.470
We have the key value storage, which is used
for reads and writes during the time critical

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:22.640
bidding process.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:31.380
Then we have traditional RDMS which is, in
that case MySQL, that we use for the more

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.700
business oriented, the standard things that
are nice to put in a database.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:40.560
So, we have our, well, ledgers and transaction
logs for money flow.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.750
Not for the high frequency stuff, but for
the more low frequency stuff.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:53.760
And then we also have a more big data type
store, which we use for doing analytics and

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:54.760
queries on.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.840
The really, really big data sets.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.030
We actually have a fourth.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:03.580
We also have our unstructured, or they're
actually structured, but we have just raw

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:10.780
logs that we can run more with MapReduce ad-hoc
queries over if everything else fails.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:15.140
Pete: And I think we spoke previously.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:19.209
You're looking at some different solutions
for NoSQL data sotres.

00:02:19.209 --> 00:02:22.660
Talk to me a little about the options there.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:29.360
Dag: Yeah, there are a lot options for an
NoSQL right now.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:40.480
And, so first of all I like the new explanation
for NoSQL, which is “not only SQL”.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:50.099
And this is all about finding the right tools
for the job and the different NoSQL solutions

00:02:50.099 --> 00:02:52.430
are usually very specialized.

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:58.180
There are some general ones like Mongo and
CouchDB that are fairly general NoSQL solutions

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:03.950
and they also they have sweet spots but in
our case we were looking at something very,

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:05.140
very simple.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:09.830
We just actually have key and values, that's
all we need.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:18.920
Something with extremely low latency and something
that performed consistently and predictably.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.830
And we looked at a number of different projects
including open source projects have actually

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:27.770
started running on open source projects.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:32.180
I'm not going to bash them right now, but
we ended up actually going for a commercial

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:33.980
vendor.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.690
Which I'm not going sell either right now.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:36.850
Well, yeah.

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:38.340
They're called Citrusleaf.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:39.340
It's a really good company, actually.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:40.480
They deserve a lot of credit.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:47.950
They took a lot of the jitter in our performance
just right out the equation, out of the equation.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:56.170
So, very, very simple yet very capable NoSQL
solution that performs insanely well.

00:03:56.170 --> 00:04:03.239
This is the stuff that we're running on our
SSD drives in a fairly small cluster, but

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:07.610
can still take immense, the throughput is
awesome.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:16.359
Pete: So you mentioned that there's faster
technology out there than the typical MapReduce,

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:20.479
Hadoop stack, that many engineers are becoming
familiar with.

00:04:20.479 --> 00:04:25.289
Talk to me a little bit more about the options
there, and what you guys are looking at.

00:04:25.289 --> 00:04:35.520
Dag: First of all, MapReduce is just a principle
on how you can process or split big data into

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:42.849
smaller chunks and then process in parallel
and then try to reduce it into something meaningful

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:43.849
in the end.

00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:49.350
And there are a lot of generalized MapReduce
frameworks, the most of well known one is

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:55.839
called Hadoop, which is basically a rather
large stack of software which is founded on

00:04:55.839 --> 00:04:58.100
the principal of MapReduce.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:01.159
We started using Hadoop.

00:05:01.159 --> 00:05:08.099
It does have some strengths as in that, it's
open source and it's fairly easy to get started

00:05:08.099 --> 00:05:11.349
with.

00:05:11.349 --> 00:05:17.300
We've had some issues running into bug It's
fairly difficult to get everything up and

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.349
running there.

00:05:19.349 --> 00:05:20.349
But it worked all right.

00:05:20.349 --> 00:05:26.220
But we were seeing, for the things we do,
we need shorter response times.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:33.409
We needed to do queries over really large
data sets, and we just found out that Hadoop

00:05:33.409 --> 00:05:34.930
didn't perform well enough for us.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:41.610
We were using Amazon's elastic MapReduce framework,
or their cloud offering, which also works

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:45.990
pretty well, but it just didn't give us the
performance we wanted.

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:54.319
And the fundamental reason for this is probably
or at least into large degree that when using

00:05:54.319 --> 00:05:57.539
Hadoop and you are using raw log files underneath.

00:05:57.539 --> 00:06:04.020
You actually have to, if you have thirty terabytes
of data, then you have to read thirty terabytes

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:05.699
off a disc.

00:06:05.699 --> 00:06:10.979
Whereas, very often, you just want to pick,
let's say, one of the queries we might run

00:06:10.979 --> 00:06:17.089
over this data set, you want to see how, what's
our over the course of a month, how many ad

00:06:17.089 --> 00:06:26.089
impressions could we sell to iPhone users
in North America, and all we're interested

00:06:26.089 --> 00:06:29.119
in them is the platform.

00:06:29.119 --> 00:06:33.430
We detect it and maybe some IP ranges or DMA
or something like that.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:34.449
But, just a couple of fields.

00:06:34.449 --> 00:06:40.229
Pete: So you're talking about selecting a
value out of just one column, out of a data

00:06:40.229 --> 00:06:43.729
set that might have many columns in the row.

00:06:43.729 --> 00:06:44.960
Dag: Yeah.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:47.099
So, that's exactly it, right?

00:06:47.099 --> 00:06:50.819
And so Hadoop is brute forcing a lot of these
things.

00:06:50.819 --> 00:06:57.949
What you really want to do is just, well,
this is just like the poster-boy for column-oriented

00:06:57.949 --> 00:07:00.300
databases.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.300
There are a few open source ones there, too.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:07.710
So, HBase is one, but Cassandra is probably
one of the more well-known there.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:09.050
HBase then Cassandra.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:13.999
There is an interesting project going on I
know that the Datastax guys are doing, with

00:07:13.999 --> 00:07:19.960
running Hadoop on top of Cassandra, which
then, at least, takes away the storage and

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:23.529
load, I/O, problem out of the equation.

00:07:23.529 --> 00:07:30.849
But it turns out that there are a lot of proprietary
commercial vendors here, that offer solutions

00:07:30.849 --> 00:07:34.960
that still just blows anything else out of
the water.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:39.479
Some of them are actually hybrid open source
as well like Infobright it's based on MySQL

00:07:39.479 --> 00:07:42.949
and has a proprietary storage engine.

00:07:42.949 --> 00:07:47.719
And has a communication, but you actually
have to.

00:07:47.719 --> 00:07:48.719
If you want it.

00:07:48.719 --> 00:07:53.889
We're not using them, but if you want these
solutions you actually have to pay for them.

00:07:53.889 --> 00:07:55.419
At least for now.

00:07:55.419 --> 00:08:02.919
And then they use compression, column-oriented
storage, and they just, it's.

00:08:02.919 --> 00:08:07.770
I recommend anybody that's looking for analytics,
and especially real-time analytics, to take

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:10.159
a look at these offerings.

00:08:10.159 --> 00:08:15.830
I know that if we were trying to do this with
something like Hadoop, well, we would have

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:16.830
made it work.

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:21.219
We could do rollups on a regular basis, all
those kinds of traditional workarounds for

00:08:21.219 --> 00:08:26.339
it, but with these solutions you can actually
just run your queries and SQL and they will

00:08:26.339 --> 00:08:31.399
respond in sub second even over hundreds and
millions of rows.

00:08:31.399 --> 00:08:34.200
Pete: And a couple of those players again
are?

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:43.479
Dag: So the ones that we've been looking at
are Vertica, Infobright, Aster Data, Greenplum.

00:08:43.479 --> 00:08:49.070
and another player called VectorWise, which
is from Ingres.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:52.900
So, they all perform incredibly well.

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:55.930
They do start to change a bit when you start
doing.

00:08:55.930 --> 00:08:59.440
So, we have two types of big data basically.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:03.590
We have the impression tracking, which is
based per campaign.

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:09.260
That's still in the region of maybe a couple
hundred million rows that we do analytics

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:10.260
on.

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:16.340
And then we have this firehose, which is in
the thirty billion row region.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:20.910
And when you start getting up to those levels
they start to differ a bit due to the way

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:26.220
they're architected, some are easy to scale
out where as others actually have to be scaled

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:27.960
up.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.200
So they're different, but they're all worth
a look.

